WEBVTT

00:00:20.200 --> 00:00:21.660
Can't sleep.

00:00:26.710 --> 00:00:28.670
Huh? What's this?

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:34.650
Thank you all for waiting!

00:00:34.650 --> 00:00:41.090
It's finally time for SucceedSoft's
big new announcement!

00:00:41.090 --> 00:00:42.810
What big new announcement?

00:00:43.280 --> 00:00:45.780
I guess I remember an announcement
about an announcement.

00:00:47.720 --> 00:00:49.650
I understand where they're coming from.

00:01:04.620 --> 00:01:05.920
Are you kidding me?

00:01:06.480 --> 00:01:11.430
Kawagoe Kyouichi, N@NA,
and Akishima Shino?

00:01:11.430 --> 00:01:13.880
They're all-stars
from the Platinum Generation!

00:01:14.360 --> 00:01:16.940
What kind of budget are they dealing with?

00:01:17.770 --> 00:01:20.010
The reaction has to be crazy...

00:01:24.250 --> 00:01:25.570
I shouldn't.

00:01:28.200 --> 00:01:32.560
I always loved things like video games,
light novels, and anime.

00:01:33.930 --> 00:01:36.240
I wanted to try working in the industry,

00:01:36.240 --> 00:01:40.270
so I quit the office job I had
and joined a bishoujo game developer...

00:01:40.780 --> 00:01:43.530
Where the workplace was more
terrible than I had ever imagined,

00:01:43.530 --> 00:01:46.480
we released games that didn't sell,
and ultimately shut down.

00:01:50.170 --> 00:01:52.330
My name's Hashiba Kyoya,
and I'm 28 years old.

00:01:52.660 --> 00:01:55.270
The kind of soon-to-be-middle-aged bachelor

00:01:55.270 --> 00:01:56.620
that you can find just about anywhere.

00:01:58.730 --> 00:02:02.260
As you might expect, I've got
no girlfriend, and no money.

00:02:02.810 --> 00:02:05.140
And now I don't even have a job...

00:02:12.600 --> 00:02:15.110
Which means I can't pay rent anymore,

00:02:17.060 --> 00:02:20.320
forcing me to pay for an express bus ticket
with the last of my cash.

00:02:21.020 --> 00:02:24.580
And here I find myself here,
back at my parents' house.

00:02:25.480 --> 00:02:30.200
Directing, video editing,
image editing, art assistant,

00:02:30.200 --> 00:02:32.710
sub-scenario writing, logo design...

00:02:33.180 --> 00:02:37.540
I tried my hand at everything I could,
and none of it amounted to anything.

00:02:38.030 --> 00:02:40.320
What was the point of it all?

00:02:55.230 --> 00:02:58.020
Akishima Shino is so amazing.

00:03:23.130 --> 00:03:23.730
Hello?

00:03:23.730 --> 00:03:26.080
Onii-chan? You doing okay?

00:03:26.080 --> 00:03:27.370
Nope.

00:03:27.370 --> 00:03:28.280
What's up?

00:03:28.280 --> 00:03:32.190
Well, I was going through my stuff
and I found something of yours.

00:03:32.190 --> 00:03:34.160
I thought I'd drop it off later.

00:03:34.570 --> 00:03:36.690
Something of mine? What?

00:03:36.690 --> 00:03:38.640
The letter from when
you got into art school.

00:03:38.640 --> 00:03:40.140
You're proud of it, right?

00:03:41.440 --> 00:03:42.850
I'll see you later, then.

00:03:50.980 --> 00:03:53.600
Art school, huh?

00:03:55.760 --> 00:04:00.710
I got into two schools,
and I wasn't sure which to attend.

00:04:01.460 --> 00:04:04.390
One was the economics department
of a regular university.

00:04:04.390 --> 00:04:08.100
The other was the visual arts
program of an art school.

00:04:09.920 --> 00:04:14.120
My admiration for creatives lead me to
take the entrance exam on a lark...

00:04:14.120 --> 00:04:16.150
But I passed somehow.

00:04:16.640 --> 00:04:18.580
But I didn't attend.

00:04:18.580 --> 00:04:20.970
I didn't have the confidence
to try a creative career,

00:04:20.970 --> 00:04:23.800
and regular college work seemed easier.

00:04:27.680 --> 00:04:31.910
I wonder what would've happened
if I had gone to art school instead.

00:04:32.530 --> 00:04:35.680
The Platinum Generation
are all the same age as me.

00:04:36.010 --> 00:04:39.040
If I had gone to art school instead,

00:04:39.040 --> 00:04:42.510
maybe I would've become
a creator, like them.

00:04:42.910 --> 00:04:46.230
I wonder if I could wake up,
and be back in time...

00:04:46.710 --> 00:04:48.430
And then I'd change things.

00:04:51.830 --> 00:04:53.180
Onii-chan?

00:04:53.730 --> 00:04:55.250
Onii-chan...

00:04:55.730 --> 00:04:57.020
Onii-chan!

00:05:00.900 --> 00:05:03.400
Would it kill you to knock—

00:05:03.400 --> 00:05:04.530
Here you go.

00:05:11.370 --> 00:05:12.240
See ya.

00:05:12.830 --> 00:05:14.850
You're welcome, by the way.

00:05:17.040 --> 00:05:20.130
I guess it doesn't work that way.

00:05:28.640 --> 00:05:31.120
There's nothing I can do now.

00:05:34.600 --> 00:05:37.330
I'd better look for a job.

00:06:00.750 --> 00:06:03.840
We wish you luck in your future endeavors.

00:06:24.540 --> 00:06:25.850
Don't do it!

00:06:33.160 --> 00:06:34.700
I am so sorry!

00:06:36.970 --> 00:06:41.160
Did I seriously look that suicidal?

00:06:42.240 --> 00:06:47.650
Well... you looked kinda lonely, maybe?

00:06:50.540 --> 00:06:51.570
I said kind of lonely—

00:06:52.100 --> 00:06:53.180
S-Sorry!

00:06:55.060 --> 00:06:57.290
Oh, sorry.

00:06:57.290 --> 00:07:01.060
When I remembered what I was worried about,
I got pissed off all of a sudden.

00:07:02.490 --> 00:07:03.940
It must be pretty serious.

00:07:03.940 --> 00:07:05.580
Tell me about it!

00:07:06.390 --> 00:07:08.950
We had staff run out on us
at a critical juncture.

00:07:08.950 --> 00:07:10.210
They ran off?

00:07:10.210 --> 00:07:13.120
Well, I work at a company
that makes games, right?

00:07:13.120 --> 00:07:17.180
Though I guess it's not really
unheard of in this industry.

00:07:19.930 --> 00:07:22.600
What are the odds that
I just stumble across someone

00:07:22.600 --> 00:07:25.630
who can direct, edit images and video,

00:07:25.630 --> 00:07:28.390
and has a general knowledge
of web development...

00:07:28.390 --> 00:07:32.510
And since I'm asking, maybe they
could hook me up with an illustrator.

00:07:33.940 --> 00:07:35.140
Uh...

00:07:35.140 --> 00:07:36.390
What?

00:07:36.740 --> 00:07:38.270
That sounds like me.

00:07:39.480 --> 00:07:40.850
It does?

00:07:41.680 --> 00:07:43.030
I think it does, anyway.

00:07:51.930 --> 00:07:57.160
I can't believe I landed
a gig at SucceedSoft.

00:08:01.380 --> 00:08:04.040
Better work hard! This is a big chance!

00:08:10.600 --> 00:08:12.870
Welcome, Hashiba Kyoya-san.

00:08:13.440 --> 00:08:17.420
I'm the head of SucceedSoft's
second development group, Kawasegawa.

00:08:17.420 --> 00:08:18.350
Nice to have you.

00:08:18.870 --> 00:08:21.480
Right! I'm very glad to be working here!

00:08:22.340 --> 00:08:24.020
She's the team head?

00:08:24.710 --> 00:08:28.600
But she's only about my age...
or even younger.

00:08:29.050 --> 00:08:30.320
Amazing.

00:08:31.530 --> 00:08:35.820
I'm going to go ahead and explain
what you'll need to get started.

00:08:35.820 --> 00:08:37.020
Right!

00:08:39.570 --> 00:08:44.030
This is the room where we've put
the reference material for our new title.

00:08:44.030 --> 00:08:46.750
But we're short-staffed,
and you can see what that's led to.

00:08:47.560 --> 00:08:51.880
I apologize you for sticking you
with basic chores for your first job, but...

00:08:51.880 --> 00:08:53.270
I'm counting on you.

00:08:56.120 --> 00:08:58.470
I shouldn't have expected too much, I guess.

00:08:59.030 --> 00:09:02.540
I'll just be grateful to even have a job.

00:09:04.820 --> 00:09:05.560
Huh?

00:09:08.710 --> 00:09:10.590
Wait, isn't this...

00:09:12.900 --> 00:09:15.310
This should probably be like this.

00:09:15.650 --> 00:09:17.090
And this like this...

00:09:17.090 --> 00:09:18.690
Let's change how this is organized.

00:09:27.030 --> 00:09:32.200
The filers were previously labeled
only with numbers.

00:09:32.660 --> 00:09:34.890
So I labeled each type of file
with a letter of the alphabet,

00:09:34.890 --> 00:09:37.840
and a number to distinguish them.

00:09:38.350 --> 00:09:42.140
I made digital copies of anything
we didn't need hard copies of,

00:09:42.610 --> 00:09:46.320
and filed the hard copies on the shelves.

00:09:46.640 --> 00:09:50.150
I made sure to label the toys
and figurines too,

00:09:50.150 --> 00:09:52.900
so now we can keep track of it all via PC.

00:09:54.980 --> 00:09:57.320
So... what do you think?

00:09:59.010 --> 00:10:02.280
Well, I have to say,
it's not what I expected.

00:10:02.680 --> 00:10:04.080
Relax, though.

00:10:04.440 --> 00:10:06.580
You exceeded those expectations.

00:10:13.740 --> 00:10:16.250
We can delay the deadline by a week,

00:10:16.250 --> 00:10:20.140
but in exchange I'd like to see whatever
you have now, even if it's still rough.

00:10:20.970 --> 00:10:23.890
That way we can get things rolling
for the backgrounds.

00:10:23.890 --> 00:10:28.480
As for your next set of specs,
getting them ready by tonight is...

00:10:30.110 --> 00:10:32.530
Sorry, it's going to have to
wait until tomorrow.

00:10:32.530 --> 00:10:34.140
If you'll excuse me.

00:10:36.510 --> 00:10:38.910
Thanks so much, Hashiba-san.

00:10:38.910 --> 00:10:42.090
I don't know what it is, but that artist tends
to lose their temper with me really fast.

00:10:42.090 --> 00:10:44.240
Your softball approach is a huge help.

00:10:44.530 --> 00:10:45.620
Not a problem at all.

00:10:45.620 --> 00:10:49.040
Just make sure to have the text
specifications ready for them.

00:10:49.330 --> 00:10:53.920
Hashiba-san! About that tutorial video
for that item we talked about yesterday...

00:10:54.280 --> 00:10:56.440
Don't worry! I've got it!

00:10:56.440 --> 00:10:59.500
I'll make sure it's ready to check
at tomorrow's morning meeting.

00:10:59.500 --> 00:11:00.830
Hashiba-san!

00:11:00.830 --> 00:11:03.900
Do you have time to edit this image later?

00:11:03.900 --> 00:11:07.170
Sure thing! I'll have it ready by tonight.

00:11:07.170 --> 00:11:09.230
Hashiba-kun, I could use a hand...

00:11:09.230 --> 00:11:11.040
Got it, I'll figure it out!

00:11:11.040 --> 00:11:13.740
Hashiba, sorry, do you have a second?

00:11:13.740 --> 00:11:14.760
Coming!

00:11:14.760 --> 00:11:20.070
Never thought someone assuming I was
jumping off an overpass would be so fortunate.

00:11:20.070 --> 00:11:22.580
I'm glad we had that misunderstanding, too.

00:11:22.580 --> 00:11:24.500
It still kind of hurts my feelings, though.

00:11:24.500 --> 00:11:26.950
And I am really sorry about that!

00:11:35.760 --> 00:11:38.720
I had a lot of worries back then.

00:11:39.560 --> 00:11:43.380
I was feeling really fed up,
which was when you saw me.

00:11:44.150 --> 00:11:47.150
It's kind of a mess
at our company, y'know.

00:11:47.150 --> 00:11:48.840
Howso?

00:11:48.840 --> 00:11:53.740
The team working on a social game
is calling us "budget parasites."

00:11:54.220 --> 00:11:57.110
And they try to screw
with our development as a result.

00:11:57.110 --> 00:11:59.870
But isn't this a big anniversary project?

00:11:59.870 --> 00:12:01.560
Why would they do that?

00:12:03.080 --> 00:12:06.200
Not everyone feels like celebrating.

00:12:06.200 --> 00:12:10.190
And not caring about the anniversary
means they think it's all a waste.

00:12:15.170 --> 00:12:19.460
But I want to make sure
this project is a success.

00:12:20.880 --> 00:12:24.730
I don't want to waste a chance to work
with the Platinum Generation.

00:12:24.730 --> 00:12:27.170
You really care about it, huh?

00:12:29.450 --> 00:12:32.480
Having you on board has been a huge help.

00:12:33.050 --> 00:12:34.560
Thank you so much.

00:12:34.560 --> 00:12:39.360
That's okay. This is
a special project for me, too.

00:12:40.720 --> 00:12:42.820
You really like Akishima Shino's art, huh?

00:12:43.800 --> 00:12:45.050
Yeah.

00:12:45.050 --> 00:12:48.060
The sooner you become
a permanent employee, the better.

00:12:48.060 --> 00:12:49.910
How are my prospects, on that front?

00:12:49.910 --> 00:12:52.150
Well, it's part of the contract.

00:12:52.150 --> 00:12:55.320
After six months, you'll be
considered for the position.

00:12:55.320 --> 00:12:57.330
I better work hard, then!

00:12:57.670 --> 00:13:01.130
I bet you want to meet
the Platinum Gen soon, too.

00:13:01.130 --> 00:13:02.240
Yeah!

00:13:15.910 --> 00:13:18.940
We haven't gotten any
more of the script yet.

00:13:18.940 --> 00:13:22.240
Having nothing show up
for a whole month isn't normal, right?

00:13:22.240 --> 00:13:24.890
I wonder what the chief thinks.

00:13:24.890 --> 00:13:27.260
She's been out a lot lately.

00:13:31.640 --> 00:13:34.250
Everyone, can I have a word?

00:13:39.440 --> 00:13:41.380
I have something I need to tell you all.

00:13:45.010 --> 00:13:46.700
The Platinum project...

00:13:50.570 --> 00:13:54.020
has been frozen, as decided
by a meeting of the board of directors.

00:13:56.160 --> 00:13:59.140
Why?! What happened?

00:14:00.290 --> 00:14:03.380
I wish I could have done more.

00:14:04.500 --> 00:14:05.810
I'm sorry.

00:14:08.150 --> 00:14:10.220
All that work, and for what?

00:14:10.220 --> 00:14:12.660
What's gonna happen to our team?

00:14:10.630 --> 00:14:13.480
Management only cares
about the social game, anyway.

00:14:12.660 --> 00:14:13.480
Are we gonna be split up?

00:14:13.480 --> 00:14:16.300
Damn it, this kills my motivation.

00:14:14.950 --> 00:14:17.010
Guess I'm drinking myself silly tonight.

00:14:17.010 --> 00:14:18.060
Again...

00:14:19.310 --> 00:14:21.460
It happened again.

00:14:48.370 --> 00:14:52.880
The team was laid off
and my contract was canceled.

00:15:04.230 --> 00:15:06.140
What did I do wrong?

00:15:11.610 --> 00:15:14.690
If I had chosen this instead...

00:15:14.690 --> 00:15:17.800
I'd be pouring my heart into creating,
brainstorming with my friends,

00:15:17.800 --> 00:15:20.430
thinking, then creating something new...

00:15:21.480 --> 00:15:24.880
And I would've met
the Platinum Generation.

00:15:24.880 --> 00:15:28.840
We could've worked together,
inspired each other...

00:15:28.840 --> 00:15:30.130
And...

00:15:30.660 --> 00:15:31.990
And then...

00:15:32.780 --> 00:15:34.980
What the hell difference does it make?!

00:15:38.350 --> 00:15:40.730
It's too late.

00:15:44.250 --> 00:15:45.730
Onii-chan?

00:15:46.210 --> 00:15:47.710
Onii-chan!

00:15:48.230 --> 00:15:49.680
Onii-chan.

00:15:52.720 --> 00:15:56.160
Finally. Were you so excited
you passed out, or something?

00:15:56.160 --> 00:15:58.890
Come on... are you being sarcastic or—

00:15:59.430 --> 00:16:03.010
Uh... since when are you into cosplay?

00:16:03.010 --> 00:16:05.490
What're you talking about?

00:16:05.490 --> 00:16:11.240
Anyway, Dad wanted to to know what
you wanted for your celebratory dinner.

00:16:11.800 --> 00:16:12.990
Celebrating?

00:16:12.990 --> 00:16:15.910
You were freaking out
about getting in before.

00:16:15.910 --> 00:16:18.650
"I got into Oonaka Arts!"

00:16:18.650 --> 00:16:21.170
That was a decade ago.

00:16:21.920 --> 00:16:24.750
Onii-chan, what are you talking about?

00:16:24.750 --> 00:16:27.630
What do you mean? I could ask you...

00:16:31.700 --> 00:16:35.150
Uh, did you borrow some of my books?

00:16:35.150 --> 00:16:36.410
Sure didn't.

00:16:36.410 --> 00:16:38.050
You always get mad when I do.

00:16:38.050 --> 00:16:42.870
But... there's only the first seven
volumes of <i>The Familiar of Zero</i>.

00:16:42.870 --> 00:16:46.980
Plus, <i>Haganai</i>, <i>Asterisk</i>,
and <i>No Game, No Life</i> are missing.

00:16:47.330 --> 00:16:48.060
Huh?

00:16:50.770 --> 00:16:54.190
Didn't we get rid of this CRT?

00:16:54.790 --> 00:16:56.140
And...

00:16:56.140 --> 00:16:59.240
Only a PreS2? What happened to the PreS3?

00:16:59.530 --> 00:17:01.340
Maybe I should use my smartphone to—

00:17:02.770 --> 00:17:04.290
A flip phone?!

00:17:12.280 --> 00:17:15.080
2006?!

00:17:21.850 --> 00:17:24.180
"Once time passes, it never comes back."

00:17:24.660 --> 00:17:27.060
"There are no second chances in life."

00:17:27.680 --> 00:17:29.990
I always believed that...

00:17:29.990 --> 00:17:33.990
But right now, what I am experiencing

00:17:34.340 --> 00:17:38.920
is the world from ten years ago that
I've been dying to go back to.

00:19:26.120 --> 00:19:32.240
I have no idea how it happened,
but I've reverted ten years into the past.

00:19:36.510 --> 00:19:38.940
It's when I was choosing
what university to attend.

00:19:39.670 --> 00:19:42.730
Where I was pondering my own future,

00:19:43.090 --> 00:19:45.680
instead of pondering
the nature of the world.

00:19:50.210 --> 00:19:52.470
I hope this is the right way?

00:19:53.210 --> 00:19:56.710
Ichisuka Higashi... Ichisuka Higashi?

00:19:56.710 --> 00:19:58.390
I take this right, then.

00:20:02.850 --> 00:20:07.970
Back then, I chose the ordinary path
because I wasn't sure of my own potential.

00:20:08.440 --> 00:20:12.420
As a result, I had some
seriously bitter experiences.

00:20:12.980 --> 00:20:14.930
Which is why, when I found myself back in time,

00:20:14.930 --> 00:20:16.860
there was only really one option.

00:20:21.170 --> 00:20:21.990
Here we go.

00:20:22.730 --> 00:20:24.320
Share House Kitayama.

00:20:30.570 --> 00:20:32.210
Excuse me?

00:20:38.170 --> 00:20:41.490
Am I the first one here?

00:20:42.730 --> 00:20:44.860
This is Akashika Movers!

00:20:44.860 --> 00:20:45.890
Coming!

00:20:45.890 --> 00:20:47.390
Thank you.

00:20:53.390 --> 00:20:55.440
All right, let's start putting stuff away.

00:21:00.350 --> 00:21:03.820
The rest can wait until tomorrow.

00:21:17.800 --> 00:21:20.320
My Dad asked me to make one promise:

00:21:20.320 --> 00:21:23.390
Since your diet can become lopsided
when you're living on your own,

00:21:23.390 --> 00:21:25.020
I have to eat plenty of fermented foods,

00:21:25.020 --> 00:21:27.290
whether it comes
from lactic acid or from natto.

00:21:28.380 --> 00:21:32.310
I really should have bought a sports
drink when I picked this up.

00:21:38.560 --> 00:21:40.700
I'm an art student now.

00:21:41.750 --> 00:21:48.610
Maybe I'll get to work together with
Akishima Shino, Kawagoe Kyouichi, and N@NA...

00:21:49.350 --> 00:21:52.490
And then... I'll finally...

00:21:53.020 --> 00:21:54.980
Finally...

00:22:02.790 --> 00:22:04.180
Hashiba-kun?

00:22:04.640 --> 00:22:06.090
Hashiba-kun...

00:22:06.510 --> 00:22:07.880
Hashiba-kun.

00:22:08.520 --> 00:22:09.820
Hashiba-kun!

00:22:11.050 --> 00:22:13.180
Hashiba-kun...

00:22:13.180 --> 00:22:16.030
What are you dozing off for, Hashiba-kun?

00:22:16.030 --> 00:22:17.790
P-President?!

00:22:17.790 --> 00:22:19.900
Am I back at my old job?!

00:22:19.900 --> 00:22:21.450
Stop daydreaming!

00:22:21.450 --> 00:22:25.230
The CG illustrations still aren't finished
for <i>Pretty Booty</i>, Hashiba-kun!

00:22:25.230 --> 00:22:26.850
Where's that damn president?!

00:22:28.600 --> 00:22:33.230
Mr. Prez, hope you're ready
to pay what you owe... with interest.

00:22:34.090 --> 00:22:36.340
H-Hang on, this has to be a mistake!

00:22:36.340 --> 00:22:37.740
Cut!

00:22:38.310 --> 00:22:40.310
That's no good, Hashiba-kun!

00:22:40.310 --> 00:22:42.500
You have to run from
the debt collectors, Hashiba-kun!

00:22:42.500 --> 00:22:43.850
President?

00:22:43.850 --> 00:22:48.850
Life isn't like a movie!
There are no second takes, Hashiba-kun!

00:22:49.880 --> 00:22:52.230
Okay, let's try this again.

00:22:52.230 --> 00:22:54.280
Take two, get ready...

00:22:54.280 --> 00:22:56.390
Because there won't be a third, Hashiba-kun!

00:22:56.390 --> 00:23:01.750
Now, run into the light!
The future is up to you, Hashiba-kun!

00:23:05.970 --> 00:23:08.950
Scene 5, Cut 12, ready...

00:23:08.950 --> 00:23:09.950
Action!

00:23:10.110 --> 00:23:11.710
Where's that damn president?!

00:23:12.930 --> 00:23:14.800
I can't let it end here!

00:23:18.890 --> 00:23:21.550
No way is that happening!

00:23:33.160 --> 00:23:35.150
What a nasty nightmare.

00:23:44.210 --> 00:23:47.360
Where have I seen this before?

00:23:50.250 --> 00:23:53.660
Oh, right, it was in an eroge we made.

00:23:54.920 --> 00:23:57.140
I guess I haven't woken up yet.

00:24:06.730 --> 00:24:08.880
Is it morning already?

00:24:16.830 --> 00:24:18.340
So cute...

00:24:23.520 --> 00:24:25.400
Y-Yes?

00:24:33.780 --> 00:24:35.590
Thirsty...

00:24:35.590 --> 00:24:37.020
Wait, I...

00:24:38.410 --> 00:24:40.830
already... started on that.

00:24:47.340 --> 00:24:50.170
Nothing beats yogurt in the morning!

00:24:50.170 --> 00:24:52.590
Yeah, I agree.

00:24:52.590 --> 00:24:56.240
Wait, hang on, who are you?

00:24:56.240 --> 00:24:59.350
Oh, right, I better introduce myself.

00:25:03.350 --> 00:25:04.770
What was that?!

00:25:04.770 --> 00:25:05.740
It was on the second floor!

00:25:08.850 --> 00:25:10.250
Hey, you okay in there?!

00:25:10.250 --> 00:25:11.210
We're coming in!

00:25:11.210 --> 00:25:13.180
W-Wait a sec!

00:25:13.180 --> 00:25:15.180
What is it? What happ—

00:25:16.760 --> 00:25:18.750
It's not what it looks like!

00:25:18.750 --> 00:25:21.950
When I woke up, she was
sleeping next to me, and—

00:25:24.070 --> 00:25:27.890
Since my futon's not here yet, I borrowed his.

00:25:27.890 --> 00:25:29.790
Wait, that's why?

00:25:38.640 --> 00:25:41.430
Okay, I'll go first, then.

00:25:41.710 --> 00:25:43.740
I'm Nanako Kogure!

00:25:43.740 --> 00:25:45.600
I'm originally from Shiga Prefecture!

00:25:45.600 --> 00:25:47.310
You can call me Nanako!

00:25:47.310 --> 00:25:48.940
Nice to meet you.

00:25:49.400 --> 00:25:51.820
She seems kinda popular,
maybe a bit of a gyaru.

00:25:51.820 --> 00:25:54.990
Let me guess: it was art school
or a life of crime—

00:25:56.030 --> 00:25:58.590
You've got a talent
for being a pain in the ass

00:25:58.590 --> 00:26:01.530
when I've only known you
for, like, 30 seconds.

00:26:01.530 --> 00:26:05.570
Let's not make fun of her, then.

00:26:05.570 --> 00:26:08.720
I'm Tsurayuki Rokuonji...

00:26:08.720 --> 00:26:10.420
I guess I'll keep it casual, too.

00:26:10.420 --> 00:26:13.290
That's a cool family name!

00:26:13.290 --> 00:26:14.260
More like pretentious.

00:26:14.260 --> 00:26:17.440
What difference does my family name make?

00:26:17.440 --> 00:26:19.890
Oh, so you're the only one
who can make fun of people?

00:26:19.890 --> 00:26:23.240
I'll go next!

00:26:23.810 --> 00:26:25.210
I'm Kyoya Hashiba.

00:26:25.210 --> 00:26:26.460
Nice to meet you.

00:26:26.460 --> 00:26:27.720
You can just call me Kyoya too—

00:26:27.720 --> 00:26:30.700
In that case, Kyoya,
I need to clarify something...

00:26:31.290 --> 00:26:32.700
Wh-What's that?

00:26:32.700 --> 00:26:36.940
Could you explain the sequence of events
between yesterday and today in detail?

00:26:36.940 --> 00:26:39.320
I already did, though!

00:26:39.320 --> 00:26:42.340
It was a coincidence!
A series of unfortunate coincidences!

00:26:42.340 --> 00:26:43.820
And, well...

00:26:44.500 --> 00:26:48.200
So you coincidentally spent the night
with a girl you just met...

00:26:48.200 --> 00:26:50.670
Before giving her a little bit
of the milky splash?

00:26:53.460 --> 00:26:55.670
Seriously, it was just a—

00:26:54.630 --> 00:26:56.300
Unbelievable!

00:26:58.150 --> 00:27:00.760
This should be interesting, I think.

00:27:02.760 --> 00:27:05.040
That just leaves me!

00:27:05.040 --> 00:27:06.460
I'm Shino Aki.

00:27:06.460 --> 00:27:09.770
I'm from Fukuoka, in Kyushu...
a town called Itoshima.

00:27:09.770 --> 00:27:14.040
Shinoaki's kind of a
masculine name, isn't it?

00:27:14.040 --> 00:27:15.690
Yeah, you're right.

00:27:15.690 --> 00:27:18.040
Aw, that ain't it though.

00:27:18.040 --> 00:27:21.070
Shino's my family name, and Aki's my name.

00:27:21.070 --> 00:27:23.120
So I'm Shino Aki!

00:27:24.770 --> 00:27:27.990
Y'all can call me Aki, too.

00:27:27.990 --> 00:27:29.440
What about Shinoaki, though?

00:27:29.440 --> 00:27:31.180
I really like the sound of Shinoaki.

00:27:31.180 --> 00:27:32.720
Shinoaki it is.

00:27:32.720 --> 00:27:36.280
Are y'all not gonna listen to me?!

00:27:37.010 --> 00:27:38.400
Shinoaki!

00:27:41.900 --> 00:27:47.140
And that was how the four of us
ended up together in this share house.

00:27:47.580 --> 00:27:49.920
The second floor's rooms were
for me and Shinoaki,

00:27:49.920 --> 00:27:52.140
with the bottom floor's being
for Tsurayuki and Nanako.

00:27:53.050 --> 00:27:56.150
We split up the chores along these lines.

00:27:56.860 --> 00:28:02.630
I won't go into all the specifics...
but it was kind of a mess.

00:28:07.010 --> 00:28:08.350
I have to say...

00:28:08.350 --> 00:28:12.250
Hey, what kinda stuff is in Shiga?

00:28:08.730 --> 00:28:12.250
Being around two girls,
and both of them being so cute...

00:28:12.250 --> 00:28:14.330
Well, there's Lake Biwa...

00:28:12.250 --> 00:28:14.350
It's like something out of a dream.

00:28:14.330 --> 00:28:15.630
Lake Biwa?

00:28:16.470 --> 00:28:17.610
What else?

00:28:17.610 --> 00:28:18.850
Other than that...

00:28:18.850 --> 00:28:20.390
And considering what's already happened,

00:28:20.390 --> 00:28:23.110
I should be careful about
anyone getting the wrong idea.

00:28:27.180 --> 00:28:30.860
Imagine that, all of us
in the Visual Arts program...

00:28:30.860 --> 00:28:32.850
Pretty wild coincidence, right?

00:28:32.850 --> 00:28:36.980
Seems great to me. We can
share notes on class, and stuff.

00:28:37.320 --> 00:28:40.280
But, man, is this really Osaka?

00:28:40.280 --> 00:28:43.110
It's more rural than it is back home.

00:28:43.110 --> 00:28:45.820
It's Osaka, but on the border
between prefectures.

00:28:45.820 --> 00:28:50.770
I hear there used to be signs warning
about pit vipers until recently.

00:28:50.770 --> 00:28:53.480
Maybe I should go home after all.

00:28:53.480 --> 00:28:55.960
If you're gonna let some
snakes intimidate you...

00:28:55.960 --> 00:28:57.960
Then this is really gonna freak you out.

00:28:58.960 --> 00:29:02.790
Oonaka Arts' signature landmark...
the Art Hill.

00:29:03.470 --> 00:29:06.330
Right... I forgot all about it.

00:29:06.330 --> 00:29:11.100
Kinda looks like the zone before
the final boss fulla tough enemies.

00:29:23.430 --> 00:29:25.950
Lotta new students
from the look of things.

00:29:26.410 --> 00:29:28.350
A whole bunch.

00:29:28.350 --> 00:29:31.370
A hundred... maybe more?

00:29:31.370 --> 00:29:32.700
Yeah.

00:29:32.700 --> 00:29:38.920
If the new students could be seated
in accordance to the numbered seats.

00:29:39.350 --> 00:29:40.590
So he says.

00:29:40.590 --> 00:29:42.420
See you all later, then.

00:29:46.210 --> 00:29:48.560
Is this 33?

00:29:51.640 --> 00:29:52.770
That's right.

00:29:52.770 --> 00:29:55.420
All right, cool. Pardon me, then.

00:29:56.600 --> 00:29:59.150
I'm finally at art school!

00:29:59.570 --> 00:30:01.950
The name's Hikawa Genkiro!

00:30:01.950 --> 00:30:04.230
You write it with the "genki"
from "How you doing?!"

00:30:04.230 --> 00:30:06.820
And the same "ro" as Ichiro
and Jiro! Genkiro!

00:30:06.820 --> 00:30:07.570
Nice to meet you!

00:30:07.570 --> 00:30:08.730
Uh...

00:30:08.730 --> 00:30:11.490
I'm Hashiba Kyoya. Likewise.

00:30:11.490 --> 00:30:15.110
So, Hashiba, did you play any sports?

00:30:15.110 --> 00:30:17.120
Not in particular, no.

00:30:17.120 --> 00:30:19.270
Just watched baseball on TV, really.

00:30:19.270 --> 00:30:23.650
Baseball, huh? Chibalotti
did great last year!

00:30:23.650 --> 00:30:27.660
And man, at last year's Koshien,
Koma-komai's Mii-kun was awesome!

00:30:27.660 --> 00:30:31.060
Oh, right... that would've
been ten years ago, huh?

00:30:31.060 --> 00:30:34.730
I guess the prince of towel's big showcase
is gonna be summer of this year...

00:30:34.730 --> 00:30:37.020
You a fan of any players, Hashiba?

00:30:37.020 --> 00:30:39.960
Uh...

00:30:39.960 --> 00:30:41.790
Maybe Oodani?

00:30:41.790 --> 00:30:45.530
Oodani? Doesn't ring any bells.

00:30:45.530 --> 00:30:47.580
You know, he's a two-way player.

00:30:47.580 --> 00:30:48.340
Who is?

00:30:48.340 --> 00:30:49.840
Oh, uh...

00:30:49.840 --> 00:30:55.040
You, uh, ever think about that neat saying,
"He who chases two hares, won't catch one"?

00:30:55.430 --> 00:30:58.710
Crap, I need to choose
my words more carefully.

00:30:58.710 --> 00:31:02.730
Now then, we have the advising
assistant professor Kano here,

00:31:02.730 --> 00:31:06.340
who has a word for all of our new students.

00:31:06.340 --> 00:31:09.580
Hey there! Nice to meet you everybody!

00:31:09.580 --> 00:31:11.750
I'm Assistant Professor Kano.

00:31:11.750 --> 00:31:14.190
Welcome to art school!

00:31:14.190 --> 00:31:15.560
That's our teacher?

00:31:15.560 --> 00:31:17.540
She seems kinda young.

00:31:18.100 --> 00:31:23.440
Now, to start out with,
I'd like to take an informal survey.

00:31:23.440 --> 00:31:28.650
Okay, first of all, everyone wants
to direct movies, raise your hand!

00:31:29.770 --> 00:31:32.910
Okay, next, who wants to be a screenwriter?

00:31:33.960 --> 00:31:35.910
Anyone want to be an animator?

00:31:36.600 --> 00:31:38.330
Game creators?

00:31:38.330 --> 00:31:42.520
There's plenty of others, right?
Filming, recording, effects, editing...

00:31:42.520 --> 00:31:44.480
All kinds of different careers!

00:31:44.920 --> 00:31:47.310
I see, I see...

00:31:50.670 --> 00:31:54.350
Okay, you all need to listen up!

00:31:55.870 --> 00:32:00.770
First of all, we had 135 graduates
from the Visual Arts program last year.

00:32:00.770 --> 00:32:05.770
How many of them do you think
landed the job they were after,

00:32:05.770 --> 00:32:07.290
at the time of their admission?

00:32:07.290 --> 00:32:09.570
You there, blondie.

00:32:16.660 --> 00:32:19.120
U-Uh...

00:32:19.670 --> 00:32:22.150
Forty-two, maybe?

00:32:22.150 --> 00:32:24.500
Bzzt! Wrong.

00:32:25.170 --> 00:32:28.460
Even that many would be like a dream, huh?

00:32:29.670 --> 00:32:33.010
The correct answer is... eight of them.

00:32:34.360 --> 00:32:36.850
Is that less than you expected?

00:32:36.850 --> 00:32:40.040
And even that is better
than the previous year.

00:32:40.990 --> 00:32:44.860
Let's change subjects, for a sec,
and talk about money.

00:32:44.860 --> 00:32:50.040
People get hungry, they get tired,
and they have to use the bathroom.

00:32:50.040 --> 00:32:53.100
We need clothes to wear,
and homes to live in.

00:32:53.100 --> 00:32:54.370
I'm talking bare necessities.

00:32:54.370 --> 00:32:58.850
And because everybody needs them,
they cost money.

00:32:58.850 --> 00:33:02.540
But... how about movies,
anime, and video games?

00:33:02.850 --> 00:33:08.080
Entertainment only becomes important
once you've got your needs met.

00:33:08.080 --> 00:33:11.500
In other words, it's low priority.
Useless, you could even say.

00:33:11.500 --> 00:33:16.010
And you all, who have come to a university
to learn to make those things...

00:33:16.010 --> 00:33:18.350
Well, that goes all
the way past eccentricity...

00:33:18.350 --> 00:33:19.660
And into stupidity.

00:33:21.020 --> 00:33:25.270
Still, if you're good enough at being stupid?
You can make money off of it.

00:33:25.270 --> 00:33:26.870
That is what you need to seek out.

00:33:26.870 --> 00:33:30.930
If you're going to be stupid anyway,
do it in a way that no one else can touch.

00:33:31.530 --> 00:33:35.290
I'm looking forward to seeing that happen.

00:33:35.630 --> 00:33:36.750
That is all!

00:33:41.770 --> 00:33:45.310
"It's not gonna be easy," in other words.

00:33:46.440 --> 00:33:50.340
Now that I think about it, these
industries are not exactly expansive.

00:33:50.650 --> 00:33:55.120
Whether it's movies, anime, or games...

00:33:55.120 --> 00:33:57.580
There's only a tiny fraction of people

00:33:57.580 --> 00:33:59.920
whose names get big enough
to become popular.

00:34:00.870 --> 00:34:04.130
Even if the people from this generation
were known as "Platinum,"

00:34:04.130 --> 00:34:07.930
it doesn't mean that every single one
of them shone bright.

00:34:07.930 --> 00:34:12.980
I should know, considering
the future I came from.

00:34:12.980 --> 00:34:16.240
But there's no sense in getting
depressed at the start.

00:34:17.670 --> 00:34:19.810
Gotta focus on getting things done.

00:34:22.370 --> 00:34:25.310
Excuse me, you dropped your student ID.

00:34:25.310 --> 00:34:27.290
Who, me?

00:34:31.850 --> 00:34:33.080
Wait, Chief?!

00:34:33.080 --> 00:34:37.420
Wait, what? I'm not in any
clubs or anything yet.

00:34:38.450 --> 00:34:39.430
Sorry.

00:34:41.430 --> 00:34:43.680
Uh... wait...

00:34:44.400 --> 00:34:47.100
Could you hand my ID over, please?

00:34:47.100 --> 00:34:48.690
Oh, right.

00:34:49.190 --> 00:34:50.190
Thanks.

00:34:56.480 --> 00:34:59.360
Huh, so she was in the same program.

00:35:07.430 --> 00:35:12.260
I might've been in the same room
as the Platinum Trio today!

00:35:16.840 --> 00:35:18.840
Scene 16, Cut 1...

00:35:18.840 --> 00:35:21.230
Ready... action!

00:35:22.460 --> 00:35:25.750
And so, our Visual Arts classes began.

00:35:25.750 --> 00:35:28.730
That said, it's not like we got
right into making movies.

00:35:29.150 --> 00:35:31.970
We observed the older students' shoots...

00:35:31.970 --> 00:35:35.570
And stuffed our heads full
of knowledge about the field.

00:35:39.220 --> 00:35:41.680
Damn it, what kind
of kaiju beats the superhero?!

00:35:41.680 --> 00:35:43.250
Sorry, heat of the moment...

00:35:43.680 --> 00:35:46.160
Freshmen, that was
an example of what not to do.

00:35:46.490 --> 00:35:49.830
Considering we were learning English verbs

00:35:49.830 --> 00:35:53.420
and secondary functions
just a few months ago...

00:35:53.420 --> 00:35:58.820
It's gonna take a while before things
like "the 180-degree rule" sink in.

00:35:59.430 --> 00:36:04.030
Although in my case,
I was 28 years old a month ago.

00:36:04.030 --> 00:36:05.830
You've got it all wrong!

00:36:06.420 --> 00:36:08.680
There's one exception, though.

00:36:08.680 --> 00:36:11.660
You're not positioned well.

00:36:11.660 --> 00:36:13.870
Give it another pause
before stepping into the scene.

00:36:13.870 --> 00:36:17.110
Camera, don't try and chase him down
with a pan. Pull out and hold.

00:36:17.620 --> 00:36:22.110
Kawasegawa was already
at the center of the action.

00:36:22.480 --> 00:36:27.010
Although she's a little
imposingly serious at times.

00:36:27.010 --> 00:36:30.520
That's the chief for you.
She's on another level.

00:36:31.000 --> 00:36:35.500
Screenplay Writing Theory

00:36:39.300 --> 00:36:41.190
Compared to that, here I am...

00:36:42.440 --> 00:36:46.100
wondering if I can really reach the level
of the Platinum Generation like this.

00:36:49.290 --> 00:36:51.430
Tsurayuki, wake up!

00:36:53.640 --> 00:36:59.320
Just a little bit more...
I'm wiped out from working all night.

00:37:00.990 --> 00:37:02.840
In spite of everything I said,

00:37:02.840 --> 00:37:06.700
the Share House Kitayama residents
seem to be taking things easy.

00:37:06.700 --> 00:37:09.450
If you wanted to be nice,
you could say they're relaxed.

00:37:09.450 --> 00:37:13.370
If you didn't, you could say they
don't seem motivated for class at all.

00:37:14.230 --> 00:37:16.120
Which is kind of a relief, to be honest.

00:37:17.210 --> 00:37:20.210
If everyone around me was like Kawasegawa...

00:37:20.730 --> 00:37:24.220
I think I might've been
afraid to attend class.

00:37:27.040 --> 00:37:30.320
Okay, when it comes to writing a script,

00:37:30.320 --> 00:37:32.720
there are ten elements you need
for things to come together.

00:37:32.720 --> 00:37:36.750
And yet you only see
eight here on the board.

00:37:36.750 --> 00:37:40.580
Seems kind of weird when
there's supposed to be ten, right?

00:37:40.580 --> 00:37:45.930
Which is why I'm gonna ask you all
what the remaining two are.

00:37:44.320 --> 00:37:47.030
Seriously?! I have no clue!

00:37:45.930 --> 00:37:47.030
Any ideas?

00:37:47.030 --> 00:37:47.990
Here.

00:37:47.990 --> 00:37:51.620
Well, someone's fired up. And you are?

00:37:52.230 --> 00:37:53.950
Kawasegawa Eiko.

00:37:53.950 --> 00:37:57.750
Is one of the missing
elements "complications"?

00:37:59.500 --> 00:38:03.290
"Complications" refers
to unpredictable turns in the story,

00:38:03.290 --> 00:38:06.220
which tend to lead into
the protagonist or hero failing.

00:38:06.730 --> 00:38:08.910
After all, no matter how
amazing an individual is,

00:38:08.910 --> 00:38:12.220
they're less interesting
without some kind of flaw.

00:38:12.590 --> 00:38:16.400
Having those faults come to the fore
during a story, and overcoming them,

00:38:16.400 --> 00:38:18.770
creates interesting drama.

00:38:21.370 --> 00:38:24.290
Correct you are. Perfect answer.

00:38:25.380 --> 00:38:26.820
She really is something.

00:38:26.820 --> 00:38:29.990
Can you tell me what the other one is, too?

00:38:29.990 --> 00:38:33.890
I'd hate to hog all the
attention. I think...

00:38:34.470 --> 00:38:37.570
I'd like to pass it off to the guy
who's snoring loudly behind me.

00:38:40.500 --> 00:38:46.130
You're an interesting one,
Kawasegawa-kun. And mean.

00:38:46.590 --> 00:38:47.800
Thank you, sir.

00:38:47.800 --> 00:38:51.050
And since you went ahead
and gave up the spotlight...

00:38:51.050 --> 00:38:53.680
Let's have sleepyhead here answer.

00:38:55.580 --> 00:38:57.730
Someone call for me?

00:38:57.730 --> 00:39:01.200
The professor did! He wants you
to name the missing element!

00:39:02.410 --> 00:39:03.690
Missing what?

00:39:10.570 --> 00:39:13.240
Well? Don't you know?

00:39:13.240 --> 00:39:15.350
If you don't, then pay more attention to—

00:39:15.350 --> 00:39:16.700
A foil.

00:39:18.650 --> 00:39:22.800
Someone who becomes the protagonist's
"enemy" in the story, in other words.

00:39:22.800 --> 00:39:24.670
It can be an individual,
or an organization...

00:39:25.290 --> 00:39:27.910
Out of the other nine
principles you've got...

00:39:27.910 --> 00:39:31.680
A simple way of putting it would be
"whoever steals the treasure."

00:39:32.360 --> 00:39:35.940
Something to help maintain tension
until the climax of the story.

00:39:35.940 --> 00:39:38.640
I figure it's a pretty important element.

00:39:39.270 --> 00:39:40.550
Is that right?

00:39:42.840 --> 00:39:45.470
You're quite the complication yourself.

00:39:45.470 --> 00:39:47.820
But you're right. Well done.

00:39:48.250 --> 00:39:49.980
Thank you, sir.

00:39:55.460 --> 00:40:00.960
I take back what I said. Looks like
I won't be able to relax, after all.

00:40:04.960 --> 00:40:06.960
It wasn't a big deal...

00:40:07.660 --> 00:40:10.920
I just recited something
I read in a book once.

00:40:10.920 --> 00:40:13.310
I don't see how that earns any praise.

00:40:13.310 --> 00:40:16.970
But that scary-looking teacher
flat-out praised you.

00:40:16.970 --> 00:40:18.670
Learn to take a compliment!

00:40:19.220 --> 00:40:22.490
He was just being nice to the freshman.

00:40:22.490 --> 00:40:25.070
Start taking that kind of thing seriously,
and you'll never get anywhere.

00:40:26.120 --> 00:40:30.560
Come on, Nanako. We've got
Visual Arts Theory for third period, right?

00:40:30.560 --> 00:40:32.110
Oh, right.

00:40:32.410 --> 00:40:34.110
See you two later.

00:40:35.970 --> 00:40:38.630
Tsurayuki-kun's really something.

00:40:38.630 --> 00:40:42.050
I don't get any of the jargon stuff,

00:40:42.050 --> 00:40:44.290
so I'm barely able to keep up.

00:40:44.290 --> 00:40:45.630
Same here.

00:40:46.780 --> 00:40:48.250
That said...

00:40:49.390 --> 00:40:53.050
I thought my extra ten years
would be an advantage.

00:40:53.050 --> 00:40:57.260
That it'd make a big difference compared
to all the other students.

00:40:58.080 --> 00:41:02.360
Even if it didn't amount to much,
I did actually work in entertainment.

00:41:02.360 --> 00:41:05.220
It was supposed to give me
some kind of advantage.

00:41:05.220 --> 00:41:07.160
But what I actually found out

00:41:07.160 --> 00:41:11.150
is that my fundamental
knowledge is severely lacking.

00:41:11.610 --> 00:41:16.110
A handful of sub-par experiences
isn't enough to equal knowledge or talent.

00:41:16.740 --> 00:41:19.100
It feels like I'm learning
that the hard way.

00:41:26.460 --> 00:41:30.430
What's wrong? You look real worried.

00:41:30.430 --> 00:41:31.630
O-Oh, no...

00:41:31.940 --> 00:41:35.380
It's nothing serious. Not really.

00:41:35.860 --> 00:41:37.570
If you say so...

00:41:38.960 --> 00:41:43.450
Come to think of it, why did Shinoaki...
apply to an arts school, anyway?

00:41:43.920 --> 00:41:47.190
She was paying close attention
in photography class, but...

00:41:47.560 --> 00:41:50.440
Maybe she wants to be a photographer?

00:41:54.150 --> 00:41:55.260
Hikawa?

00:41:55.260 --> 00:41:56.240
Yo!

00:41:56.240 --> 00:41:58.470
Why are you dressed like that?

00:41:58.470 --> 00:42:01.610
Come on, it's my club uniform!

00:42:01.610 --> 00:42:03.250
A... uniform?

00:42:03.250 --> 00:42:05.940
Hell yeah! There's only a handful
of them in the whole world...

00:42:05.940 --> 00:42:07.140
A ninja club!

00:42:07.140 --> 00:42:10.710
It's the Society for the Study
of Ninjutsu! I joined.

00:42:10.710 --> 00:42:12.780
I think I've heard of that, actually.

00:42:12.780 --> 00:42:15.610
In Japan, I think Oonaka Arts has the only one.

00:42:15.610 --> 00:42:18.220
Yeah! Nice and exclusive, know what I mean?

00:42:18.220 --> 00:42:22.010
Plus, one of the older
kunoichi girls is super hot.

00:42:22.520 --> 00:42:24.930
I bet that's why he joined.

00:42:24.930 --> 00:42:27.670
You sure are having fun, huh, Hikawa-kun?

00:42:27.670 --> 00:42:31.450
Hell yeah! You two should check
out some extracurricular stuff, too.

00:42:31.450 --> 00:42:32.940
It's seriously fun!

00:42:38.880 --> 00:42:42.450
Are these the club rooms?

00:42:42.450 --> 00:42:44.860
Seems like it.

00:42:45.910 --> 00:42:48.710
Nobody's home, though.

00:42:50.080 --> 00:42:51.500
Hey, hey!

00:42:52.690 --> 00:42:54.330
Up here, you two!

00:42:55.810 --> 00:42:57.340
You guys freshmen?

00:42:57.640 --> 00:42:58.900
Uh, yes.

00:42:58.900 --> 00:43:00.400
We are.

00:43:00.400 --> 00:43:03.760
I knew it! That settles things, then.

00:43:05.640 --> 00:43:11.020
The 2006 freshman mixer
is now officially underway!

00:43:11.020 --> 00:43:12.360
Cheers!

00:43:18.190 --> 00:43:23.560
We didn't even decide on a club
to join... Is this okay?

00:43:23.560 --> 00:43:25.530
I figure it is.

00:43:25.530 --> 00:43:28.930
They never asked us if we were in one!

00:43:30.080 --> 00:43:31.460
True enough.

00:43:53.270 --> 00:43:54.940
Say, Shinoaki...

00:43:55.480 --> 00:43:58.280
What do you want to do?

00:44:29.180 --> 00:44:31.250
Oh, you're awake?

00:44:32.430 --> 00:44:34.400
Kyoya-kun...

00:44:34.400 --> 00:44:36.200
Oh, sorry!

00:44:36.200 --> 00:44:38.030
It's fine. Don't worry about it.

00:44:38.390 --> 00:44:42.710
We've been busy non-stop
since the entrance ceremony, after all.

00:44:52.680 --> 00:44:57.960
You're pretty sharp, Kyoya-kun.
It's hard to believe we're the same age.

00:44:59.040 --> 00:45:01.210
You really think so?

00:45:01.210 --> 00:45:04.560
Yeah! You pay a lot
of attention to everybody.

00:45:04.560 --> 00:45:07.840
And you're calmer'n most
20-year-olds I know.

00:45:08.490 --> 00:45:13.200
But sometimes you look like you're
tired from thinking too hard.

00:45:13.770 --> 00:45:17.610
At lunch today, you looked real worried.

00:45:18.950 --> 00:45:21.740
It's nothing serious, though. I mean it.

00:45:22.400 --> 00:45:24.030
Ow, ow, ow...

00:45:24.030 --> 00:45:26.250
Quit trying to brush me off and talk!

00:45:26.680 --> 00:45:29.160
You might feel better that way.

00:45:33.430 --> 00:45:36.800
Well, today... seeing Tsurayuki in class...

00:45:36.800 --> 00:45:40.670
It made me realize how little I can do.

00:45:41.260 --> 00:45:43.680
No... it's not just him.

00:45:44.010 --> 00:45:47.430
Everybody at this school has something
that makes them special.

00:45:47.830 --> 00:45:51.810
But I've got nothing. So...

00:45:52.430 --> 00:45:56.440
I was thinking... about how little I can do.

00:45:56.740 --> 00:45:59.420
And it had me worrying a lot.

00:46:00.500 --> 00:46:05.850
That surprises me, because
I figured you could do anything.

00:46:05.850 --> 00:46:07.440
I can't, though.

00:46:09.210 --> 00:46:13.470
But you can cook
and keep yourself fed, right?

00:46:13.470 --> 00:46:14.270
Yeah...

00:46:14.270 --> 00:46:18.050
And do laundry, clean the house,
and keep things nice, right?

00:46:18.050 --> 00:46:19.330
I can do that.

00:46:19.330 --> 00:46:23.010
You can make plans
and actually stick to them, right?

00:46:23.010 --> 00:46:24.350
That too.

00:46:24.350 --> 00:46:28.340
I can't do any of that.

00:46:28.340 --> 00:46:32.600
I'm jealous of people
who can, if I'm being honest.

00:46:33.110 --> 00:46:37.160
Tsurayuki-kun probably feels the same way.

00:46:37.160 --> 00:46:41.900
There's bound to be stuff
he's jealous of you for.

00:46:42.970 --> 00:46:48.850
Folks who can't do anything
are all trying to find what we can do.

00:46:48.850 --> 00:46:54.210
So I bet even the people
you think are amazing

00:46:54.210 --> 00:46:56.120
are just as worried as you are.

00:47:06.390 --> 00:47:08.510
There, there.

00:47:11.540 --> 00:47:13.940
Shinoaki... listen...

00:47:16.350 --> 00:47:17.600
Thanks.

00:47:19.270 --> 00:47:22.230
I better do my best, too, huh?

00:47:23.760 --> 00:47:28.140
What can I do?

00:47:32.690 --> 00:47:34.080
We're home.

00:47:34.980 --> 00:47:37.060
I guess they're both at work.

00:47:37.060 --> 00:47:40.520
I'm gonna head to bed, then.

00:47:40.520 --> 00:47:41.350
Yeah.

00:47:43.100 --> 00:47:44.700
Our next report...

00:47:44.700 --> 00:47:50.150
A man was reported for causing
a scene in Dotonbori.

00:47:48.670 --> 00:47:52.100
I'm exhausted for today.
I'll work on my assignment tomorrow.

00:47:50.150 --> 00:47:52.090
When approached for a...

00:47:52.450 --> 00:47:54.000
I think I'll take a bath.

00:47:56.120 --> 00:47:59.100
Oh, I forgot to hand it to her.

00:48:02.820 --> 00:48:04.560
I wonder if she's asleep already.

00:48:07.360 --> 00:48:09.650
Shinoaki? Are you awake?

00:48:32.770 --> 00:48:37.130
Akishima Shino... is Shinoaki.

00:48:37.130 --> 00:48:42.850
Folks who can't do anything
are all trying to find what we can do.

00:48:43.220 --> 00:48:48.480
I see. That's what she wants to do, then.

00:48:50.800 --> 00:48:53.730
I need to find something, too.

00:48:55.590 --> 00:48:59.120
What do I want to do?

00:49:00.140 --> 00:49:02.720
And what is it that I can do?
